    // through the backward walk onward, and the ENTIRE forward walk,
    // failing with "Cannot access a disposed context instance" /
    // "disposed object... sqlite3_stmt". Root cause: this used to run
    // against AutoFetch above, which is injected per-circuit (lives only
    // as long as this browser tab's Blazor Server connection does). This
    // operation can run for a long time - long enough that the tab's
    // connection can drop (sleep, a flaky network, the tab just being
    // closed) before it finishes, and once that circuit is gone, ASP.NET
    // Core disposes everything scoped to it - including the DbContext
    // this was still running SaveChanges against on a background thread.
    // Fix: give this one operation its OWN DI scope (same pattern
    // BackgroundJobsService.RunOnceAsync uses for its hourly tick) so its
    // DbContext's lifetime is tied to the operation itself, not to
    // whether this tab stays open.
